WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.862
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.862 --> 00:00:10.492
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Need me to sit right here?

00:00:10.492 --> 00:00:11.918
&gt;&gt; Yep, that's right.

00:00:11.918 --> 00:00:13.788
So Ariana welcome to Stanford.

00:00:13.788 --> 00:00:16.607
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much,
what a fabulous auditorium.

00:00:16.607 --> 00:00:17.905
&gt;&gt; We handpicked them
specifically just for you.

00:00:17.905 --> 00:00:22.997
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So you often quote a Persian poet

00:00:22.997 --> 00:00:29.041
who encouraged us to live our lives as
though they were rigged in our favor.

00:00:29.041 --> 00:00:32.948
Now as it is often easy to
proclaim the fact after success,

00:00:32.948 --> 00:00:37.996
it appears as though you actually
embodied this mindset from an early age.

00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:40.736
But not just you, your parents, too.

00:00:40.736 --> 00:00:44.380
So, I was wondering if you could take
us back to Greece where it all began.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:49.218
And share with the audience a story
as to how your parents met and

00:00:49.218 --> 00:00:51.511
how they came to be in Greece.

00:00:51.511 --> 00:00:58.364
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all Liv, thank you so
much for starting with my favorite quote,

00:00:58.364 --> 00:01:02.270
which I carry with me
laminated in my wallet.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:08.102
And which I keep bombarding my children
with, in fact my oldest daughter,

00:01:08.102 --> 00:01:12.095
Christina is here, so
she's heard it many times.

00:01:12.095 --> 00:01:16.038
It really did all start with my mother,

00:01:16.038 --> 00:01:22.501
because we lived in a one bedroom
apartment in Athens, Greece.

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:28.777
And one day, I saw a picture in
the magazine of Cambridge University.

00:01:28.777 --> 00:01:34.161
And I said to everybody who could listen,
I really want to go there.

00:01:34.161 --> 00:01:38.654
And everybody said don't be
ridiculous you don't speak English.

00:01:38.654 --> 00:01:40.217
We don't have any money and

00:01:40.217 --> 00:01:44.845
it's really competitive to get into
Cambridge even if you're an English girl.

00:01:44.845 --> 00:01:49.212
But my mother said,
let's see how you can get there, and

00:01:49.212 --> 00:01:53.955
that involved going to the British
council and taking my GCS.

00:01:53.955 --> 00:01:57.018
Which as you know since
you're an Oxford girl,

00:01:57.018 --> 00:02:01.241
it's what you need to take as
the first step and borrowing Money.

00:02:01.241 --> 00:02:05.609
And then one thing which I kind of love,
is that she said, one day,

00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:09.530
I got us these cheap tickets and
we can go and see Cambridge.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:13.231
It was just kind of an early
visualization trip.

00:02:13.231 --> 00:02:16.547
We're not going to see
anybody at Cambridge.

00:02:16.547 --> 00:02:21.308
We're just, we're going to see Cambridge
and kind of make it more real for

00:02:21.308 --> 00:02:22.905
me that I could go there.

00:02:22.905 --> 00:02:26.899
And to cut a long story
short I got an exhibition,

00:02:26.899 --> 00:02:31.978
which is kind of a scholarship,
a version and a scholarship.

00:02:31.978 --> 00:02:35.987
I remember getting the telegram
saying awarded exhibition and

00:02:35.987 --> 00:02:39.021
I have to find out what
the hell was exhibition.

00:02:39.021 --> 00:02:45.994
[LAUGH] And to Cambridge and
that definitely changed my life.

00:02:45.994 --> 00:02:51.909
It was kind of the unexpected trajectory

00:02:51.909 --> 00:02:58.001
that made a lot of other things possible.

00:02:58.001 --> 00:03:04.346
Especially because I fell in love with
a Cambridge union, the Debating Society.

00:03:04.346 --> 00:03:12.251
And I really, really put a lot of
my energy into learning to speak.

00:03:12.251 --> 00:03:17.070
And whenever anybody says,
well I can't speak or

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:23.156
I'm terrified of speaking,
apparently public speaking is,

00:03:23.156 --> 00:03:28.575
in terms of fears,
higher up than death by mutilation.

00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:30.945
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Okay, so this is not good for
me right now, then.

00:03:30.945 --> 00:03:33.253
Okay, good to know.

00:03:33.253 --> 00:03:37.851
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So I started literally
by writing down every word.

00:03:37.851 --> 00:03:40.438
I would be called in
sometimes after midnight.

00:03:40.438 --> 00:03:44.952
And because I was so bad and
also had this heavy accent which was even-

00:03:44.952 --> 00:03:45.913
&gt;&gt; Accents are good.

00:03:45.913 --> 00:03:46.486
[LAUGH] Accents are good.

00:03:46.486 --> 00:03:48.668
&gt;&gt; Well maybe now, maybe in America.

00:03:48.668 --> 00:03:52.253
But you know perfectly well,
not in England.

00:03:52.253 --> 00:03:57.191
Anyway, so [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You've

00:03:57.191 --> 00:04:01.806
got to take whatever card you can play so
I'll take the British accent here.

00:04:01.806 --> 00:04:05.677
&gt;&gt; So I spent an enormous
amount of time at the union.

00:04:05.677 --> 00:04:10.390
In fact, I spent more time at
the union than I spent studying,

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:14.938
which is why you got a first at Oxford and
I only got a two one.

00:04:14.938 --> 00:04:18.822
&gt;&gt; Okay, and that's why you're sitting
in that chair and I'm right here.

00:04:18.822 --> 00:04:25.486
&gt;&gt; Well, you are 3,000 years younger so.

00:04:25.486 --> 00:04:30.748
&gt;&gt; So on that point, in Thrive You,
one of your best selling books,

00:04:30.748 --> 00:04:36.794
you talk about having this inner voice,
that sort of obnoxious neighbor.

00:04:36.794 --> 00:04:38.067
And yet, from the outside,

00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:40.624
you look as though you have
this remarkable self belief.

00:04:40.624 --> 00:04:44.715
Because you not only went to Cambridge but
despite a glorious accent,

00:04:44.715 --> 00:04:47.501
you decided to run for
president of Cambridge.

00:04:47.501 --> 00:04:53.628
And became the first foreign lady or
foreign lady at all to become President.

00:04:53.628 --> 00:04:58.763
That continued thereafter whereby
after your first successful book,

00:04:58.763 --> 00:05:02.112
your next one was
rejected 36 times before.

00:05:02.112 --> 00:05:07.111
And then even with the Huffington Post,
you launched it to a backdrop of critics.

00:05:07.111 --> 00:05:11.908
So this perseverance appears to be
a common thread throughout your journey.

00:05:11.908 --> 00:05:16.083
How much of that spun from
drive of being the underdog, or

00:05:16.083 --> 00:05:19.915
was it a mindset that you
taught yourself over time?

00:05:19.915 --> 00:05:24.306
&gt;&gt; Well, it was a mindset that
was very much something that

00:05:24.306 --> 00:05:26.743
my mother kept talking about.

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:31.388
One of her favorite sayings was failure
is not the opposite of success.

00:05:31.388 --> 00:05:33.650
It's a stepping stone to success.

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:38.181
But the obnoxious roommate,
that voice of doubt and

00:05:38.181 --> 00:05:41.586
self-judgment, remains very loud.

00:05:41.586 --> 00:05:43.871
And people don't write about it.

00:05:43.871 --> 00:05:46.664
But I can tell you, I can still hear it.

00:05:46.664 --> 00:05:49.308
I can still still hear, for example,

00:05:49.308 --> 00:05:53.285
when I gave my first big speech at the,
what do they call?

00:05:53.285 --> 00:05:55.222
Do they still call it a paper speech?

00:05:55.222 --> 00:05:57.344
&gt;&gt; They do indeed, yeah,
traditions die hard.

00:05:57.344 --> 00:06:01.873
&gt;&gt; Meaning that you actually are one of
the main speakers as opposed to somebody

00:06:01.873 --> 00:06:04.158
they call on after the main speakers.

00:06:04.158 --> 00:06:09.342
And I was speaking on
the same side as JK Galbraith

00:06:09.342 --> 00:06:15.171
against William Buckley and
I remember every detail.

00:06:15.171 --> 00:06:18.390
And the topic of
the speech was The Market,

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:22.158
meaning the flea market is a snare and
a delusion.

00:06:22.158 --> 00:06:27.023
And I was sitting next to Galbraith and
while Buckley was speaking,

00:06:27.023 --> 00:06:31.048
Galbraith leaned over to me and
said, can you stand up.

00:06:31.048 --> 00:06:35.938
You have the right at the union to stand
up and every speaker has to give way for

00:06:35.938 --> 00:06:37.710
the point you want to make.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.622
So can you stand up, he said,
and interrupt him and make it.

00:06:40.622 --> 00:06:45.392
And he mentioned some kind of
recondite econometric point,

00:06:45.392 --> 00:06:49.891
which I stupidly stood up and
interrupted Buckley with.

00:06:49.891 --> 00:06:52.474
And then he turned to me and
said, well madam,

00:06:52.474 --> 00:06:56.273
I don't know what market you patronize and
brought the house down.

00:06:56.273 --> 00:06:58.915
And I sat down feeling
completely humiliated,

00:06:58.915 --> 00:07:01.495
which would have been neither here nor
there.

00:07:01.495 --> 00:07:05.637
Except the obnoxious roommate, for
the next week, I'm not exaggerating.

00:07:05.637 --> 00:07:09.917
Was replaying that incident
as though this was the end,

00:07:09.917 --> 00:07:12.661
not just of my career, but my life.

00:07:12.661 --> 00:07:16.396
And looking back, of course,
it is none of the above,

00:07:16.396 --> 00:07:19.661
but that's what
the obnoxious roommate does.

00:07:19.661 --> 00:07:24.562
And it kind of basically tears you apart,
and I've had so

00:07:24.562 --> 00:07:27.119
many experiences like that,

00:07:27.119 --> 00:07:32.758
where all my energy would be
absorbed in listening to that voice.

00:07:32.758 --> 00:07:37.451
And that's why I've spent a lot
of of time in my life trying to

00:07:37.451 --> 00:07:40.871
evict the obnoxious roommate from my head.

00:07:40.871 --> 00:07:44.865
And I'm doing a pretty good job now but
it took many, many years.

00:07:44.865 --> 00:07:49.525
And it changes your life when you
don't have to waste your energy

00:07:49.525 --> 00:07:54.293
dealing with that voice but
you can actually go on with your life.

00:07:54.293 --> 00:07:58.361
And recognize that sometimes thing
are not going to go well, and

00:07:58.361 --> 00:08:03.276
sometimes you're not going to perform
at your best and that's part of life.

00:08:03.276 --> 00:08:08.050
It's funny you say that because I read
somewhere that you appear to be in your

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:10.150
own person from a very young age.

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:13.585
Would you mind sharing the story
about your fifth birthday party?

00:08:13.585 --> 00:08:17.770
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yes I always love to read.

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:20.730
I love to lose myself in books.

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:26.166
And so from my fifth birthday party,
my mother had invited my best friends.

00:08:26.166 --> 00:08:30.230
And I told them they had to go home
because they were interrupting my reading.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:35.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:38.490
&gt;&gt; Yeah well this is clearly a passion and

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:44.730
although you've written 15 books to date
now across a wide variety of topics.

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:49.190
It seems to be politics that's kind
of captured your attention the most.

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:52.180
What is it that drew you to the political
sphere, especially in the discussion and

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:52.800
commentary side?

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:58.250
&gt;&gt; So, it was politics and
as the Dean mentioned,

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:02.940
the Third World America book was

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:07.810
very much sort of my deep concern about
what was happening in the country.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:11.630
And how we are not paying
sufficient attention, and

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:15.940
that remains a very deep concern of mine.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:23.290
But, I now feel increasingly that
the thing we are most starved for

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:27.269
in our political lives as well as
the rest of our lives is wisdom.

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:34.390
And I feel that we're kind of drowning
in data and starved for wisdom.

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:38.200
And so my passion now is,
how can we connect

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:43.180
with that part of ourselves that
is the wisest part of ourselves?

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:49.630
And whether we are politicians or
business leaders or media leaders.

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:52.460
There are huge problems in front of us.

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:56.550
And if we don't connect to that part,
we are going to be operating and

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:58.080
making decisions from the surface.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:05.970
And as a result missing the opportunity
to really come up with big solutions.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:08.440
And I was fascinated by

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:14.390
how leaders that we admire throughout
history have done that in the past.

00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:19.435
Like one of my favorite stories that I've
included in my new book, is about FDR.

00:10:19.435 --> 00:10:26.553
In 1940, when there was all this
pressure on him to enter the war.

00:10:26.553 --> 00:10:31.910
But the American public was completely
opposed to it, so he didn't literally,

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:36.978
he didn't know what to do, and what he
did would never have happened today.

00:10:36.978 --> 00:10:44.525
He took ten days off and
went on a naval ship around the Caribbean.

00:10:44.525 --> 00:10:48.810
And Eleanor would send him
letters saying things,

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:54.090
I'm so happy to think of you sleeping and
refueling.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:59.560
And his aids afterwards have
written how he truly refueled and

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:01.870
in the process of refueling.

00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:05.960
He came up with what was a political
masterpiece of a solution.

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:12.000
The land lease program that allow
the America to enter their war and

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.820
at the time when otherwise public opinion
would have been completely opposed to it.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:18.910
And I feel we all need to do that.

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:24.000
And we need to validate people
who do that as opposed to judging

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:29.920
politicians who gone vacation or
who play golf.

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:34.410
Who do anything to refuel and
therefore ideally come up with

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:37.835
the solutions if they are really
at the heart of leadership.

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:43.050
&gt;&gt; It's interesting because some may say
that the reason that it doesn't happen

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:46.530
is because of this 24/7 media commentary.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:51.314
And increasingly it's thought
in some circles that media is

00:11:51.314 --> 00:11:56.680
increasingly shaping politics as
opposed to just reporting of it.

00:11:56.680 --> 00:12:00.669
So, how do you think through and try and
control that with your curation and

00:12:00.669 --> 00:12:01.818
editorial prowess?

00:12:01.818 --> 00:12:03.341
So you mean it's our fault, right?

00:12:03.341 --> 00:12:05.630
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was trying to be diplomatic.

00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:06.410
Some they say.

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:07.979
Certainly not myself but some.

00:12:07.979 --> 00:12:12.450
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I think we first of all,

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:18.670
we are not going to avoid the 24/7
nature of media and the internet.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:22.520
This is whether you,
even if you never go the Huffington Post,

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:25.650
or the New York Times site, or any site.

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:30.060
You have your Facebook and
Instagram and endless social media.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:34.580
So that's going to be the nature
of our lives going forward.

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:40.359
So that's why it's even more imperative
that we learn to set our own boundaries.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:46.889
And that we need to
stop giving in to FOMO,

00:12:46.889 --> 00:12:52.157
the fear of missing out
&gt;&gt; We don't know if that is here totally.

00:12:52.157 --> 00:12:54.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because in the end what

00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:56.074
we're missing out on is our life.

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:02.880
And that is something which will
require a major culture shift.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:07.030
I mean we can do it individually and
a lot of people are doing it.

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:11.820
And ironically there is now more and
more technology that paradoxically

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:15.800
is helping us deal with technology.

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:22.491
But in the end, it will require
some support from the culture,

00:13:22.491 --> 00:13:26.659
whether it's culture at school,
at college,

00:13:26.659 --> 00:13:31.054
and the workplaces where we are and
our families.

00:13:31.054 --> 00:13:34.576
So I feel that we are in the process
now of a major culture shift,

00:13:34.576 --> 00:13:37.114
and we're in the middle
of this transition.

00:13:37.114 --> 00:13:41.928
And like any other transition,
a lot of different types of

00:13:41.928 --> 00:13:47.050
behavior are valued and
not exhibited, all at the same time.

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:52.201
And whether you're optimistic or
pessimistic depends on what you focus on.

00:13:52.201 --> 00:13:55.464
&gt;&gt; So this Fourth Revolution
has been a big topic at Davos

00:13:55.464 --> 00:13:57.870
amongst many of the world leaders.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:01.650
And you ran for
governor of California once but

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:05.930
haven't returned to
politics directly yourself.

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:09.782
What caused you to run then and
why not since and

00:14:09.782 --> 00:14:12.572
would you like to bring
about the 4th resolution?

00:14:12.572 --> 00:14:20.094
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] No, I have,
I would rather have five hour root canal.

00:14:20.094 --> 00:14:25.917
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There's

00:14:25.917 --> 00:14:30.402
very few things that I can categorically
say but I would never run for office is

00:14:30.402 --> 00:14:34.550
one of them which I'm sure Christina
my daughter will be happy to hear.

00:14:34.550 --> 00:14:38.880
She was very opposed to my
running in the first place.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:42.100
I would never run,
first of all because I feel

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:47.530
I can contribute more with what I'm doing.

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:52.200
And secondly, because politics at
the moment is completely poisoned and

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:56.800
I think that's one of the problems
that really we got to the point where

00:14:56.800 --> 00:15:01.898
anybody who can do anything else is not
going to enter political life anymore.

00:15:01.898 --> 00:15:05.870
So we would definitely need
to change the system so

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:10.800
that we can bring in
better people to run but

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:14.920
I am definitely not
going to be one of them.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:17.760
Maybe you, maybe you decide to-
&gt;&gt; Well you'll certainly do without

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:20.420
me bumbling along and
making a mess of things.

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:25.980
Okay, so let's talk about the platform
from which you're currently able to

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:28.500
shape the view, the Huffington Post.

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:33.730
So you established this back in the day,
it was a revolutionary online platform,

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:37.590
mix or original content,
aggregation and blogging.

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:42.470
Yet you were at the time neither
part of the media establishment nor

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:46.540
a common man that the bloggers
represented nor a tech person.

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:51.115
So how did the idea come about and how
did you get it off the ground initially?

00:15:51.115 --> 00:15:55.852
&gt;&gt; So the idea really came
about because I could see that

00:15:55.852 --> 00:15:59.621
the conversation was moving online, and

00:15:59.621 --> 00:16:04.466
I wanted to sort of Elevate
the online conversation,

00:16:04.466 --> 00:16:07.588
because as you said, at the time,

00:16:07.588 --> 00:16:12.973
bloggers were perceived as
people who couldn't get a job,

00:16:12.973 --> 00:16:17.523
in their pajamas,
in their parent's basement.

00:16:17.523 --> 00:16:21.767
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I want to demonstrate that,

00:16:21.767 --> 00:16:24.520
in fact, it could be different.

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:28.700
And so from the beginning,
my aspiration for Huff was,

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:33.640
remember we started with five people
out of my home in Los Angeles, but my-

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:35.165
&gt;&gt; In the basement, in their pajamas.

00:16:35.165 --> 00:16:37.245
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No, not in the basement,

00:16:37.245 --> 00:16:42.009
a rather nice office but [LAUGH] but
still, a tiny operation.

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:48.944
But the goal was to ultimately become
both a journalistic enterprise

00:16:48.944 --> 00:16:53.805
of the kind that we did become,
we now have 850 reporters,

00:16:53.805 --> 00:16:58.599
editors, engineers in 15 countries,
we won a Pulitzer.

00:16:58.599 --> 00:17:03.132
So, to become a really substantive
journalistic enterprise, but

00:17:03.132 --> 00:17:05.460
to also be a platform.

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:11.164
And that was just as
important from the beginning.

00:17:11.164 --> 00:17:17.950
And on day one,
I had an amazing roster of bloggers.

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:23.139
I had emailed everybody I knew and
had said if there is anything on your mind

00:17:23.139 --> 00:17:28.680
that you want to write about it, please
write about it on the Huffington Post.

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:31.088
And we're going to make it super easy for
you.

00:17:31.088 --> 00:17:36.165
I know all of you could be writing for
The New York Times,

00:17:36.165 --> 00:17:40.271
because I invited people
like Larry David and

00:17:40.271 --> 00:17:45.024
Ellen DeGeneres and,
this is how long ago it was, so

00:17:45.024 --> 00:17:49.158
many of them are dead now,
Walter Cronkite.

00:17:49.158 --> 00:17:53.970
[LAUGH]
And basically,

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:59.250
we had this amazing first day
when a lot of these people,

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:04.150
everyone I just mentioned, was on the
first day of the Huffington Post blogging.

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:09.450
So suddenly, blogging was elevated
into something which you could do,

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:12.440
even though you could
actually write an op-ed

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:16.880
that would be accepted in
the Financial Times or The New York Times.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:21.470
And the reason why they would do that
is because it was so much easier.

00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:24.599
They would just write something and
we would post it and

00:18:24.599 --> 00:18:28.205
they wouldn't be edited and
they wouldn't go back and forth,

00:18:28.205 --> 00:18:31.290
asking them to make it shorter or
longer or whatever.

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:35.080
And, in fact,
we created a little concierge service

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:38.210
where people in LA could
literally dictate.

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:41.840
They could call us from
the phone from their cars and

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:46.450
we would take dictation and then send
it back to them to make any edits.

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:50.188
And I think Larry David, everything he
wrote, he would dictate from the set.

00:18:50.188 --> 00:18:54.880
Ari Emanuel, who I remember, call me from
the golf course, and say I have this

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:59.190
idea about Mel Gibson, it's terrible
that Hollywood isn't going against him.

00:18:59.190 --> 00:19:01.611
Remember when he made his
anti-semitic comments and

00:19:01.611 --> 00:19:03.123
Hollywood was giving him a pass?

00:19:03.123 --> 00:19:07.234
And, literally, Ari called me on a Sunday,
I took dictation, and

00:19:07.234 --> 00:19:10.263
I sent it back to him,
he made a couple of changes,

00:19:10.263 --> 00:19:13.530
we posted it and
it changed the climate in Hollywood.

00:19:14.585 --> 00:19:19.414
Suddenly, Disney announced we're not going
to be doing his next movie, etc., etc.

00:19:19.414 --> 00:19:23.419
So people began to see
the power of blogging and

00:19:23.419 --> 00:19:29.386
the power of just expressing an opinion,
having it up immediately.

00:19:29.386 --> 00:19:33.981
We had, from the beginning,
our comments were pre-moderated,

00:19:33.981 --> 00:19:39.160
because I wanted to keep a civil
environment and avoid ad hominem attacks.

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:43.441
Because I felt like I was inviting
these people to come to my home and

00:19:43.441 --> 00:19:45.122
express their views, and

00:19:45.122 --> 00:19:49.724
I owed it to them that nobody was
going to suddenly start trashing them.

00:19:49.724 --> 00:19:55.331
And we had very little money, we started
the Huffington Post with $2 million,

00:19:55.331 --> 00:20:00.870
which I collected from my friends, and
my partner, Kenny Lerer, collected.

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:04.490
Actually, my first investors
were Laurie and Larry David and

00:20:04.490 --> 00:20:08.518
when they got divorced they split
[LAUGH] the Huffington Post investment.

00:20:08.518 --> 00:20:14.440
[LAUGH] And so
I remember paying my daughter's $5 each

00:20:14.440 --> 00:20:20.525
to moderate comments,
they were in their early teens.

00:20:20.525 --> 00:20:25.871
[LAUGH] And basically we used everything
we could to create from the beginning

00:20:25.871 --> 00:20:30.390
a civil environment in which these
people would want to return.

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:35.119
And probably the breakthrough
moment was when,

00:20:35.119 --> 00:20:39.970
who was the identity of
deep throat was revealed.

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:42.018
Do you remember that?

00:20:42.018 --> 00:20:49.610
And Nora Ephron was in high demand,
everybody wanted her to tell her story.

00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:53.743
What did she know, when did she know it,
because at the time she was,

00:20:53.743 --> 00:20:56.687
because she had been
married to Carl Bernstein.

00:20:56.687 --> 00:21:00.369
And so she calls me up and
she says, you know what,

00:21:00.369 --> 00:21:05.279
I don't want to go to CNN and
get in makeup and I don't want to have to

00:21:05.279 --> 00:21:10.760
bother with a New York Times op-ed,
I'm sending you my blog.

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:16.774
So the fact that Nora Ephron chose
this new platform to write something,

00:21:16.774 --> 00:21:19.141
which was then everywhere,

00:21:19.141 --> 00:21:24.663
made people realize that if it was
something that was newsworthy,

00:21:24.663 --> 00:21:31.193
they could write in the Huffington Post,
then it would be everywhere anyway.

00:21:31.193 --> 00:21:35.539
CNN and the New York Times and everybody
would be compelled to link to it,

00:21:35.539 --> 00:21:38.330
because that was the only
way to get the story.

00:21:39.338 --> 00:21:44.480
So that was when we really
kind of arrived in a way,

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:47.660
in terms of people coming to us to write,

00:21:47.660 --> 00:21:51.900
and that has remained kind
of a big passion of mine.

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:55.212
Recruiting people,
interesting voices, in fact,

00:21:55.212 --> 00:21:59.994
I want to invite all of you to write on
the Huffington Post, and I always make it

00:21:59.994 --> 00:22:04.647
super easy by giving you my email address
so you can email it to me directly.

00:22:04.647 --> 00:22:10.177
Now, we give people we trust passwords,
so you can post directly,

00:22:10.177 --> 00:22:16.192
and then our editors ,front page and
social, and we translate the best,

00:22:16.192 --> 00:22:20.890
so my email address is
arianna@huffingtonpost.com.

00:22:20.890 --> 00:22:23.883
That's one of the good things when
you found a company you get a good

00:22:23.883 --> 00:22:24.641
email address.

00:22:24.641 --> 00:22:29.255
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So we are now up to

00:22:29.255 --> 00:22:34.857
100,000 bloggers and
our goal is get to a million.

00:22:34.857 --> 00:22:39.738
We're creating a lot of new
technology that will make

00:22:39.738 --> 00:22:44.190
it easier to post and
to navigate the site, etc.

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:48.741
&gt;&gt; Good luck getting sleep after 500,
600 people email you their blogs.

00:22:48.741 --> 00:22:52.439
But before we move on,
just on the point of the platform,

00:22:52.439 --> 00:22:55.521
given that it was always
meant as this medium for

00:22:55.521 --> 00:22:59.777
anyone to share their opinion and
voice pretty much unfiltered.

00:22:59.777 --> 00:23:04.482
What led to the decision to share your
name with it, knowing that your personal

00:23:04.482 --> 00:23:09.403
reputation will be associated with the
material posted on the website, and how,

00:23:09.403 --> 00:23:12.570
if at all,
has that effected the curation process?

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:15.830
&gt;&gt; Well, I was always, like all in.

00:23:15.830 --> 00:23:20.244
[LAUGH] There was never any
question of my separating

00:23:20.244 --> 00:23:23.230
myself from the Huffington Post.

00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:27.670
I have to say that my daughters begged
me not to call it the Huffington Post,

00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:31.900
because they thought it was just going
to be an embarrassing failure and

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:35.016
they would not be able to
show their faces at school.

00:23:35.016 --> 00:23:37.876
So I remember tearful
moments with Christina and

00:23:37.876 --> 00:23:42.841
Isabella saying you can't call it the
Huffington Post, that's so embarrassing.

00:23:42.841 --> 00:23:47.273
[LAUGH] And Isabella, my younger daughter,

00:23:47.273 --> 00:23:52.791
announced that she will never blog,
and she hasn't.

00:23:52.791 --> 00:23:57.468
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so that, for me,

00:23:57.468 --> 00:24:00.717
it was all like saying, I'm all in.

00:24:00.717 --> 00:24:07.656
This is it and And curation though has
always been a very important part of

00:24:07.656 --> 00:24:14.255
it from the beginning even though
the platform was open we have ground

00:24:14.255 --> 00:24:19.400
rules like we will not
publish conspiracy theories.

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:23.060
Like if you think 9/11 was an inside job,

00:24:23.060 --> 00:24:25.600
we're not going to publish
it on the Huffington Post.

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:29.880
We're not going to publish
cookie medical theory.

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:35.960
So we create a set of boundaries,

00:24:35.960 --> 00:24:40.520
that you may disagree with
people who are writing but

00:24:40.520 --> 00:24:43.595
it's not going to be
because they are insane.

00:24:43.595 --> 00:24:46.252
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's always helpful,

00:24:46.252 --> 00:24:48.555
a good boundary to have
in life in general.

00:24:48.555 --> 00:24:53.530
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, some of your recent books lament

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:59.410
the lack of surprise in the modern world
and yet, I know that this is a reason from

00:24:59.410 --> 00:25:05.310
the fact that an online content is
filtered based on preexisting preferences.

00:25:05.310 --> 00:25:09.990
So how would you think through the fact
that some platforms similar to

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:14.680
Huff Post are responsible for
creating this bubble effect and

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:17.260
kind of narrowing our preferences and
are you

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:21.860
thinking of ways to introduce serendipity
again online as browsing once did.

00:25:21.860 --> 00:25:25.890
&gt;&gt; Well, right now actually,
what is happening is that

00:25:27.130 --> 00:25:33.430
the majority of people don't receive that
information by going to a destination but

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:38.790
through social, even the heart of
the Post, which is probably the last

00:25:38.790 --> 00:25:44.150
big destination site, and
that's now the majority of

00:25:44.150 --> 00:25:49.060
our traffic through social Through people
posting something that they've read and

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:53.290
happened to post on Facebook,
or tagging their friends.

00:25:53.290 --> 00:26:00.410
This is kind of a revolution
in how we create a site.

00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:06.200
I tried to convince friends of mine,
for example, who.

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:10.045
I started a new business and
I invested a lot of money on the website.

00:26:10.045 --> 00:26:13.724
How useless that is.

00:26:13.724 --> 00:26:17.350
[LAUGH]
I actually say to them

00:26:17.350 --> 00:26:21.160
that if I was launching the Huffington
Post today there would be no website

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:24.090
because it's all social.

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:30.080
And I think one of the reasons
why we succeeded at remaining at

00:26:30.080 --> 00:26:34.910
the forefront of this business is
because we changed our strategy.

00:26:34.910 --> 00:26:37.550
So three years ago,

00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:44.080
because I woke up after eight hours
sleep feeling completely recharged,

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:48.150
I could see the iceberg that was
about to hit the Titanic And

00:26:48.150 --> 00:26:52.510
the iceberg was that people were not
going to be coming to the Huffington Post

00:26:52.510 --> 00:26:56.730
directly in the large numbers they were
coming to the Huffington Post directly.

00:26:56.730 --> 00:27:01.528
We had, our operation was being
driven very much around our Splashes.

00:27:01.528 --> 00:27:07.190
We launched the idea of the Splash early

00:27:07.190 --> 00:27:12.080
on in the life of the Huffington Post,
which was again very radical and

00:27:12.080 --> 00:27:16.820
controversial at the time because the idea
was that you needed to put as many stories

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:22.260
as possible at the top, kind of above
the fall, to drive traffic to them.

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:22.970
And we said, no.

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:30.010
We are going to announce what we consider
is the most important story of the moment.

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:33.170
We may change it ten times in
the course of the day, but

00:27:33.170 --> 00:27:35.540
it created a sense of drama.

00:27:35.540 --> 00:27:41.620
It made people keep wanting to come
back to see what's the big story now.

00:27:41.620 --> 00:27:47.400
And we hired editors
who really knew how to

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:53.720
create amazing headlines that were
a little playful and not conventional

00:27:53.720 --> 00:28:00.370
One of my favorites was when
Dominique Strauss-Kahn was arrested.

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:02.375
Our headline was OMG, IMF.

00:28:02.375 --> 00:28:06.971
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:28:06.971 --> 00:28:11.150
so the point, though,

00:28:11.150 --> 00:28:16.510
was as things change
&gt;&gt; We decided

00:28:16.510 --> 00:28:21.300
to stop prioritizing
our other front pages.

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:25.940
We still prioritize the front page because
we're still getting enough traffic to it.

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:32.210
But we stop prioritizing the splashes
of what by then we had 70 sections

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:38.970
at the Huffington Post covering everything
from, Colleges to wellness, to business.

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:43.070
And editors would spend a lot of time
creating beautiful splashes which would be

00:28:43.070 --> 00:28:49.440
seen by fewer and fewer people,
so we, made different teams

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:55.520
that instead worked on how
to make content be social.

00:28:55.520 --> 00:28:56.380
And be viral.

00:28:56.380 --> 00:28:58.070
But here's another thing we discovered.

00:28:58.070 --> 00:29:02.230
Normally we think of content
going viral as being

00:29:02.230 --> 00:29:07.340
cats on skateboards or cute animals, and

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:12.890
we discovered that the content that goes
most viral is content around solutions.

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:17.870
Things that people want to share
&gt;&gt; Positive stories, yeah.

00:29:17.870 --> 00:29:24.360
&gt;&gt; And so we have now focused our
coverage in three main areas,

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:31.360
you know, the first is obviously news and
politics, where we take our own,

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:37.120
you know, I would consider our job
to take stands We don't consider it

00:29:37.120 --> 00:29:42.530
our jobs simply to blindly kind
of just write what's happening,

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:44.430
because we feel you can
get that anywhere now.

00:29:45.990 --> 00:29:48.790
Why come to the Huffington Post
if you just want to know who won

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:50.180
the Wisconsin race?

00:29:50.180 --> 00:29:53.599
First of all, you'd have gotten
it from your Twitter feed.

00:29:55.020 --> 00:30:00.020
So for us, I mean, just one of the most
controversial thing we did that

00:30:00.020 --> 00:30:04.530
everyday that passes,
I consider one of our best decisions, was

00:30:05.540 --> 00:30:10.025
covering, at the beginning,
Donald Trump in the entertainment section.

00:30:10.025 --> 00:30:17.480
[LAUGH]
And then on the day that he announced

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:22.742
that his proposal to ban 1.6 million,

00:30:22.742 --> 00:30:26.950
billion Muslims from this
country to start covering him

00:30:26.950 --> 00:30:32.200
under a clear present danger
who then editor's note appended

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:36.920
Underneath each story were his name
appears that says, we would like to remind

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:42.560
our readers that Donald Trump regularly
advocates balance at his lies.

00:30:42.560 --> 00:30:44.550
And is a birth.

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:45.440
Which people forget.

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:49.890
People forget that his still believes
Obama was not born in this country.

00:30:49.890 --> 00:30:52.750
Wants to ban at one point
six billion Muslims,

00:30:52.750 --> 00:30:56.050
a serial liar, misogynist and xenophobe.

00:30:56.050 --> 00:30:58.370
So-
&gt;&gt; Quite subtle.

00:30:58.370 --> 00:30:58.965
&gt;&gt; It's very subtle.

00:30:58.965 --> 00:31:04.720
[LAUGHTER] So basically we have
no opinion on Donald Trump.

00:31:04.720 --> 00:31:05.600
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:10.920
&gt;&gt; And really, I think the key here
was that in a way, you think how can

00:31:10.920 --> 00:31:15.950
you cover both entertainment then a clear
and present danger, king Kim Jong Un,

00:31:15.950 --> 00:31:20.720
you know, somebody who is
both a buffoon and a danger.

00:31:20.720 --> 00:31:24.510
The two things are not contradictory, and
the two of them really exemplify that

00:31:24.510 --> 00:31:29.410
fact, so that's one bucket
The other bucket is solutions and

00:31:29.410 --> 00:31:32.750
why we have a dedicated
section called What's Working

00:31:32.750 --> 00:31:37.360
who brought in a great journalist from
The Guardian Jo Confino to run it.

00:31:37.360 --> 00:31:39.810
And in every area whether it's business,

00:31:39.810 --> 00:31:44.860
the environment, workplaces,
we focus on what is working.

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:50.145
What are the solutions that are working
and this is the content that goes more

00:31:50.145 --> 00:31:55.940
viral than anything, it's absolutely
amazing and the third market is Wellness.

00:31:55.940 --> 00:32:00.900
&gt;&gt; Just on that point again, a lot of the,
even through your Facebook feed and

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:04.934
so forth, comes again from
your Preconceived tendencies.

00:32:04.934 --> 00:32:08.825
So when certain articles pop up,
for example, someone searches on

00:32:08.825 --> 00:32:13.672
the immigration crisis, would it not be a
case of having on one side, the story, but

00:32:13.672 --> 00:32:16.539
then on the other,
something like and by the way,

00:32:16.539 --> 00:32:21.014
here are the great immigrants that build
America like Steve Jobs was Syrian?

00:32:21.014 --> 00:32:23.653
And so forth and having them side
by side such that it's almost

00:32:23.653 --> 00:32:26.770
put in front of every reader,
regardless of what they search for.

00:32:26.770 --> 00:32:31.260
&gt;&gt; Well absolutely we would be
covering the basics of the story.

00:32:31.260 --> 00:32:34.260
This is not kind of
an alternative to that.

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:41.150
But so often media cover the crisis, the
problem, without also covering the fact

00:32:41.150 --> 00:32:46.980
that There are solutions to most problems
that are actually happening somewhere.

00:32:46.980 --> 00:32:49.500
But they are just not scaled enough.

00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:52.580
So, I know you do a lot of work here.

00:32:52.580 --> 00:32:54.153
But how do you scale something good?

00:32:54.153 --> 00:32:58.579
So we feel that by putting
the spotlight on why this is working,

00:32:58.579 --> 00:33:00.256
we can help them scale.

00:33:00.256 --> 00:33:06.600
We talk about copycat crimes,
how about copycat solutions?

00:33:06.600 --> 00:33:11.090
So I think that for us, the larger
point here is reimagining journalism.

00:33:12.100 --> 00:33:13.900
So that in journalism schools,

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:17.510
right now you are taught
that if it bleeds, it leads.

00:33:18.850 --> 00:33:23.258
And of course if anybody's unfortunate
enough to watch local new,

00:33:23.258 --> 00:33:28.828
you would've thought that the only things
going on the communities are burglaries,

00:33:28.828 --> 00:33:32.797
rapes, murders, and
this is just the choice of the outlet.

00:33:32.797 --> 00:33:39.056
They think that's the way to drive people
to their broadcast, but I think the fact

00:33:39.056 --> 00:33:45.237
that now most people receive their news
through social is changing the dynamic.

00:33:45.237 --> 00:33:50.032
&gt;&gt; So, on the topic of solutions,
a lot of your current book is around

00:33:50.032 --> 00:33:55.250
kind of helping us as individuals
become better at what we do.

00:33:55.250 --> 00:33:59.960
Now what many may not know given your long
string of successes is the continuous

00:33:59.960 --> 00:34:03.950
stream of personal hurdles that
went on in the background, right?

00:34:03.950 --> 00:34:08.640
You very unfortunately had complications
with your first pregnancy,

00:34:08.640 --> 00:34:11.520
you had a breast cancer scare, I believe.

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:15.850
You had a public divorce,

00:34:15.850 --> 00:34:19.900
within the family, certain issues
around substance abuse and so forth.

00:34:21.370 --> 00:34:24.910
And yet you continue to go on and
be successful.

00:34:24.910 --> 00:34:30.570
To what extent was work a distraction
to help get through these moments?

00:34:30.570 --> 00:34:35.134
And why was it that it took a literal bump
to your head to get you to kind of wake

00:34:35.134 --> 00:34:39.639
up and reevaluate, and now help us to
avoid that situation in the future?

00:34:39.639 --> 00:34:41.078
Sorry for the question.

00:34:41.078 --> 00:34:41.705
&gt;&gt; No, that's great.

00:34:41.705 --> 00:34:49.490
I think that work was never a distraction
because I always kind of loved my work.

00:34:49.490 --> 00:34:53.763
I think what lead to my
collapse in 2007 and

00:34:53.763 --> 00:34:57.599
my breaking my cheekbone on the way down,

00:34:57.599 --> 00:35:02.421
was that I had completely
bought into the collective

00:35:02.421 --> 00:35:07.366
delusion that burnout was
the way to achieve things.

00:35:07.366 --> 00:35:12.168
That yes, I know I'm exhausted and
I know I'm kind of sleep walking

00:35:12.168 --> 00:35:16.563
through my life but hey,
I'm building the Huffington Post.

00:35:16.563 --> 00:35:20.396
And I had the delusion that so
many entrepreneurs have.

00:35:20.396 --> 00:35:23.623
That nothing could be done except by me.

00:35:23.623 --> 00:35:27.336
[LAUGH] And therefore,
I had to be up all the time and

00:35:27.336 --> 00:35:31.936
driving this third baby that I
considered the Huffington Post

00:35:31.936 --> 00:35:36.712
while my first baby, Christina,
was on college tour with me and

00:35:36.712 --> 00:35:42.829
she had asked rightly that I should not be
on my Blackberry while we were together.

00:35:42.829 --> 00:35:46.777
Remember, that's 2007, so
just a Blackberry time.

00:35:46.777 --> 00:35:52.611
And when she would go to sleep,
I would start working and

00:35:52.611 --> 00:36:00.020
to cut a long story short,
I ended up back at home collapsing.

00:36:00.020 --> 00:36:02.030
But in a way I feel very, very grateful.

00:36:02.030 --> 00:36:04.830
Speaking going back to Roomie and

00:36:04.830 --> 00:36:07.710
living life as if everything
is rigged in your favor.

00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:10.280
I honestly believed I
would either be dead or

00:36:10.280 --> 00:36:13.830
have had a heart attack if
I had continued that way.

00:36:13.830 --> 00:36:18.010
Because these things become cumulative,
so the price you pay,

00:36:18.010 --> 00:36:21.590
it's not obvious right away.

00:36:21.590 --> 00:36:25.790
Things seem to be working
until they stop working.

00:36:25.790 --> 00:36:29.900
And the great thing about
last year was that it was

00:36:29.900 --> 00:36:34.714
the year when executive after
executive collapsed, and

00:36:34.714 --> 00:36:38.436
either died or
ended up with a heart attack.

00:36:38.436 --> 00:36:41.385
Most recently the CEO of United Airlines.

00:36:41.385 --> 00:36:48.310
And if you read his schedule before
his collapse, you can see why.

00:36:48.310 --> 00:36:53.260
He's back at work now but
he ended up with a major heart attack.

00:36:53.260 --> 00:36:56.631
The CEO of BMW collapsed
during a press conference.

00:36:56.631 --> 00:37:03.272
The head of MMA at JP Morgan,
Jimmy Lee, died on his treadmill.

00:37:03.272 --> 00:37:08.453
And I mean, I could go on and
on and what is interesting

00:37:08.453 --> 00:37:14.212
is that we've kind of convinced
the world that exercise and

00:37:14.212 --> 00:37:19.163
nutrition are important,
whether we do it or not,

00:37:19.163 --> 00:37:23.350
we kind of recognize they're important.

00:37:23.350 --> 00:37:25.230
But the third leg of the stool is sleep.

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:31.600
And you have people who
are literally put the alarm on and

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:35.440
they wake up exhausted,
then drag themselves to the gym.

00:37:35.440 --> 00:37:39.370
Which now every sleep scientist, and you
have some of the best here at Stanford,

00:37:39.370 --> 00:37:43.890
will tell you it's just a terrible mistake
for your health and for your weight.

00:37:43.890 --> 00:37:47.530
If all you care about is your weight,
sleep in.

00:37:47.530 --> 00:37:51.848
Don't wake up sleep deprived and go the
gym, because your body is going to crave

00:37:51.848 --> 00:37:57.410
carbs and sugars in order to be
able to function during the day.

00:37:57.410 --> 00:37:59.690
&gt;&gt; It is somewhat ironic
that in the Valley,

00:37:59.690 --> 00:38:05.320
we have sheer panic attacks when our
iPhone depletes in energy down to like 7%.

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:06.663
But the fact that we're charging and

00:38:06.663 --> 00:38:08.750
running around at 2% doesn't
seem to affect us at all.

00:38:08.750 --> 00:38:12.734
&gt;&gt; That is such a great point in fact,
if you think of it,

00:38:12.734 --> 00:38:17.796
we do take better care of our
smartphones then we take of ourselves.

00:38:17.796 --> 00:38:21.333
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because as you said I actually begin to

00:38:21.333 --> 00:38:24.100
panic at about 13%, is yours 7?

00:38:24.100 --> 00:38:26.260
Mine barely gets turned on so I'm okay.

00:38:26.260 --> 00:38:30.980
&gt;&gt; I normally travel with about three
portable chargers just in case.

00:38:30.980 --> 00:38:35.670
And yet, if they had asked me the day
I collapsed, Ariana, how are you?

00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:37.880
I would have said fine.

00:38:37.880 --> 00:38:40.200
Because exhaustion had
become the new normal.

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:44.689
And do you know now,
that if you go on Google,

00:38:44.689 --> 00:38:49.519
at least when I last checked a week ago,
and you type out why am I,

00:38:49.519 --> 00:38:53.480
the most common auto-complete
is why am I so tired?

00:38:53.480 --> 00:38:58.565
And the second most common
auto-complete is why am I always tired?

00:38:58.565 --> 00:38:59.090
&gt;&gt; So, so

00:38:59.090 --> 00:39:03.998
I like your mantra of encouraging
us all to sleep our way to the top.

00:39:03.998 --> 00:39:10.540
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's not my words, credit goes to you.

00:39:13.580 --> 00:39:17.990
Obviously, it's all good and well, kind
of raising awareness among the rank and

00:39:17.990 --> 00:39:22.350
file, but the challenge is
the environment in which we operate.

00:39:22.350 --> 00:39:27.280
So to what extent to your point around
all these CEOs kind of waking up and

00:39:27.280 --> 00:39:29.180
literally smelling the coffee,

00:39:29.180 --> 00:39:33.960
do you think that there should be an
equivalent of the giving pledge for sleep?

00:39:33.960 --> 00:39:39.887
Whereby you kind of shame everyone into
getting rid of that social stigma,

00:39:39.887 --> 00:39:44.778
writing on paper their commitment
to adopt the third metric,

00:39:44.778 --> 00:39:48.824
embody a work environment
which rewards people for

00:39:48.824 --> 00:39:54.301
coming in rested, auto-deletes
emails when they're on holiday.

00:39:54.301 --> 00:39:58.102
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Gives you taxis to get home at night

00:39:58.102 --> 00:40:01.633
when you're sleep deprived and-
&gt;&gt; What are you doing when you graduate?

00:40:01.633 --> 00:40:03.212
&gt;&gt; Working on this.

00:40:03.212 --> 00:40:06.476
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] These are brilliant ideas.

00:40:06.476 --> 00:40:13.570
I love the equivalent of the giving
pledge, let's talk about that afterwards.

00:40:13.570 --> 00:40:17.450
I think this is a really
fantastic idea because really,

00:40:17.450 --> 00:40:22.210
we are talking about the need for
a culture shift, and there are pioneers,

00:40:22.210 --> 00:40:27.100
who are leading the way, and
removing some of the stigma.

00:40:27.100 --> 00:40:32.170
When, for example, Satya Nadella,
when he became the CEO of Microsoft,

00:40:32.170 --> 00:40:37.200
told Business Insider that he sleeps for
eight hours, and

00:40:37.200 --> 00:40:42.380
could not be an effective CEO if he did
not, that was pretty revolutionary.

00:40:42.380 --> 00:40:46.500
Because in the past, and
when I say in the past,

00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:49.693
I mean a year ago, before he said it,

00:40:49.693 --> 00:40:55.259
even if a CEO was sleeping eight hours,
he would never admit it.

00:40:55.259 --> 00:41:02.277
Because the whole idea was the Jon Bon
Jovi song that I'll sleep when I'm dead,

00:41:02.277 --> 00:41:06.269
or you lose you snooze,
you snooze you lose.

00:41:06.269 --> 00:41:11.122
Or congratulating people for
working 24/7, which now sleep scientists

00:41:11.122 --> 00:41:15.375
will tell you is the cognitive
equivalent of coming to work drunk but

00:41:15.375 --> 00:41:17.780
that has been the prevalent culture.

00:41:18.850 --> 00:41:23.910
And now, I mean, actually, last week,
[COUGH] too late to include it

00:41:25.010 --> 00:41:28.710
in the book, was a real tipping point.

00:41:28.710 --> 00:41:34.780
And that was a McKinsey
study that they extracted

00:41:34.780 --> 00:41:38.298
part of the Harvard Business Review, I
don't know why they didn't do it for you.

00:41:38.298 --> 00:41:40.770
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And [COUGH].

00:41:40.770 --> 00:41:41.433
&gt;&gt; We were busy sleeping.

00:41:41.433 --> 00:41:45.463
&gt;&gt; A mistake, a clear mistake,
which the title first of all,

00:41:45.463 --> 00:41:50.155
when you saw the title, you would
have thought it was from The Onion.

00:41:50.155 --> 00:41:56.360
The title was The Proven Link Between
Effective Leadership and Sleep.

00:41:57.680 --> 00:42:05.900
And it was co-written By the Mackenzie
Chief Global Learning Officer and

00:42:05.900 --> 00:42:10.900
a woman who was identified as
Mackenzie's sleep specialist.

00:42:10.900 --> 00:42:15.170
The idea that Mackenzie has
a sleep specialist, to teach you

00:42:15.170 --> 00:42:20.630
not how to do without sleep, but
to teach you the importance of sleep.

00:42:20.630 --> 00:42:25.040
Shows that we are in
an amazing site guys moment.

00:42:25.040 --> 00:42:31.110
And once I stop kind of pinching myself,

00:42:31.110 --> 00:42:35.740
I celebrated the part of this had happen
and reach out of course to the people who

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:39.759
wrote it to have them write about it on
having them first, which they're doing.

00:42:40.940 --> 00:42:46.820
And incidentally, the sleep specialist
has now been hired by Facebook

00:42:46.820 --> 00:42:49.080
to give these workshops at Facebook.

00:42:50.510 --> 00:42:54.750
So this is all an amazing new phenomenon
and what I love about this piece,

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:56.320
which I highly recommend,

00:42:56.320 --> 00:43:01.110
is that they walk you through what
happens to the prefrontal cortex.

00:43:01.110 --> 00:43:03.782
Which is where the leadership functions,

00:43:03.782 --> 00:43:07.120
that's what it called the executive
functioning is housed.

00:43:07.120 --> 00:43:11.550
And how incredibly degraded they
are when they are sleep deprived.

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:20.180
And then I was co-hosting
Squawk Box on Tuesday

00:43:20.180 --> 00:43:24.570
and I asked Mark Marburg one of the good
things about co-hosting and he said you

00:43:24.570 --> 00:43:29.620
can invite people that you want to bring
these messages to the financial community.

00:43:29.620 --> 00:43:33.780
And I invited Mark Bertolini,
the CEO of Aetna

00:43:33.780 --> 00:43:38.680
because I wanted him to talk about the new
initiative that he started at Aetna.

00:43:40.180 --> 00:43:44.465
And the third largest
health insurance company,

00:43:44.465 --> 00:43:50.484
which is he asked his employees to
track their sleep with the Fitbit and

00:43:50.484 --> 00:43:56.620
anyone who gets at lease seven hours
a night gets $25 for each night.

00:43:58.020 --> 00:44:01.980
And that's kind of again
an amazing statement,

00:44:01.980 --> 00:44:04.330
by this sea of health insurance company.

00:44:04.330 --> 00:44:07.332
And then he has Duke tracking the results.

00:44:07.332 --> 00:44:12.160
And they are finding a 7%
reduction in healthcare costs

00:44:12.160 --> 00:44:15.596
in all the wellness programs they have.

00:44:15.596 --> 00:44:21.070
And a 62-minute a week
improvement in productivity.

00:44:21.070 --> 00:44:24.960
So it really helps dispel
all these illusions that

00:44:26.340 --> 00:44:30.080
in order to be productive you
have to cut down on sleeps.

00:44:30.080 --> 00:44:32.660
&gt;&gt; So I hope all my professors
are listening because I often email them

00:44:32.660 --> 00:44:36.130
saying terribly sorry it's late,
I overslept, and they're like Leigh,

00:44:36.130 --> 00:44:39.192
the class is at 3:30, and
I was like I was having a nap, you know?

00:44:39.192 --> 00:44:40.610
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now I can just forward them and

00:44:40.610 --> 00:44:42.360
tell them I was decreasing my study.

00:44:42.360 --> 00:44:43.650
&gt;&gt; You're increasing your performance.

00:44:43.650 --> 00:44:44.550
&gt;&gt; Exactly, exactly.

00:44:44.550 --> 00:44:45.705
&gt;&gt; Is that this afternoon?

00:44:45.705 --> 00:44:46.900
&gt;&gt; No.

00:44:46.900 --> 00:44:49.140
&gt;&gt; I have a conversation here at Stanford,

00:44:49.140 --> 00:44:51.185
if you have woken up from your nap,
you going to come.

00:44:51.185 --> 00:44:53.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I set the alarms.

00:44:53.400 --> 00:44:59.000
&gt;&gt; With Ingrid Iguodala,
the MVP from the Golden State Warriors,

00:44:59.000 --> 00:45:04.550
and the conversation's being
introduced by Emmanuel Mignot,

00:45:04.550 --> 00:45:07.290
Head of your Stanford Sleep Center.

00:45:07.290 --> 00:45:11.760
&gt;&gt; And he's going to talk about
how sleep changed his game.

00:45:11.760 --> 00:45:16.250
And he has tracked it,
he has all the data.

00:45:16.250 --> 00:45:21.350
And he in fact, Instagrammed himself
holding his MVP while sleeping.

00:45:21.350 --> 00:45:24.410
Because he feels he only got that because.

00:45:24.410 --> 00:45:29.610
His game is so dramatically improved
when he started getting enough sleep.

00:45:29.610 --> 00:45:35.040
And I love pointing to athletes,
because all they care about winning.

00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:38.940
So, those in business school
who only care about winning.

00:45:38.940 --> 00:45:43.170
I hope there's nobody here like that, but
let's say, that's all you care about,

00:45:43.170 --> 00:45:45.010
succeeding, winning.

00:45:45.010 --> 00:45:45.890
&gt;&gt; And lose weight.

00:45:45.890 --> 00:45:47.045
&gt;&gt; Getting to the top.

00:45:47.045 --> 00:45:48.470
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:45:48.470 --> 00:45:50.650
&gt;&gt; Sleep is a performance
enhancement tool.

00:45:51.850 --> 00:45:53.520
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
Well clearly I've been dozing a bit on

00:45:53.520 --> 00:45:56.160
stage because we haven't
got that many minutes left,

00:45:56.160 --> 00:45:59.480
but we'll turn to the audience for
some questions, and

00:45:59.480 --> 00:46:03.740
they'll be two microphones roaming
with Kara at the back there to start.

00:46:03.740 --> 00:46:06.600
&gt;&gt; Great,
we'll start with a question from Twitter

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:08.200
&gt;&gt; What do you think is the future for

00:46:08.200 --> 00:46:09.530
online content creation?

00:46:11.550 --> 00:46:16.060
&gt;&gt; Well, the future is incredibly bright.

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:22.750
I think what's happening now is
that we are all content creators,

00:46:22.750 --> 00:46:27.520
it's not just publishers who
are content creators, anybody can

00:46:28.600 --> 00:46:33.330
be a content creator whether
you're a student and

00:46:33.330 --> 00:46:36.650
entrepreneur or a brand.

00:46:36.650 --> 00:46:39.870
In fact brands increasingly now,

00:46:39.870 --> 00:46:44.710
have entire departments that
are about content creation and

00:46:45.790 --> 00:46:49.980
we're talking earlier backstage
about native advertising.

00:46:49.980 --> 00:46:54.720
For us of their having their posts,
native advertising and

00:46:54.720 --> 00:46:58.690
it has become a major
source of monetization.

00:46:58.690 --> 00:47:03.720
And what we have finding is that
we have a separate department,

00:47:03.720 --> 00:47:05.770
separate from editorial.

00:47:05.770 --> 00:47:08.900
But informed by the same beliefs.

00:47:08.900 --> 00:47:12.760
And standards that creates contents for
brands.

00:47:12.760 --> 00:47:19.370
We, for example,
made a very profitable idea

00:47:19.370 --> 00:47:25.240
with Sleep Number, which is a mattress
company with embedded technology.

00:47:25.240 --> 00:47:28.462
And we have a dedicated section,

00:47:28.462 --> 00:47:33.650
it is transparently labeled
as a sponsored section.

00:47:33.650 --> 00:47:37.067
But again, as we're discussing backstage,

00:47:37.067 --> 00:47:41.796
that doesn't stop people from
wanting to consume the content,

00:47:41.796 --> 00:47:46.454
provided the content is good,
credible, and well produced.

00:47:55.416 --> 00:47:56.301
&gt;&gt; Hi, Arianna.

00:47:56.301 --> 00:47:58.560
Thanks so much for joining us.

00:47:58.560 --> 00:48:04.171
There is an article out today in
the New York Times playing on the growing

00:48:04.171 --> 00:48:09.221
wealth disparity in this country
that also says that the richest

00:48:09.221 --> 00:48:14.006
people in the United States far
outlive the poorest people.

00:48:14.006 --> 00:48:17.870
This is not necessarily new but
that gap is widening.

00:48:17.870 --> 00:48:23.200
Part of the reason for this is stress and
unhealthy living habits.

00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:27.293
And so I wonder what you're doing as
you're promoting this message of wellness,

00:48:27.293 --> 00:48:31.029
including sleep, that there are a lot
of people who can't partake in, and

00:48:31.029 --> 00:48:32.710
that's not accessible to them.

00:48:32.710 --> 00:48:37.972
So how do we make sure that we're
not just continuing to grow

00:48:37.972 --> 00:48:45.140
this divide where those of us in this room
get to be healthy and happy and wealthy.

00:48:45.140 --> 00:48:48.377
And there are a lot of people who
don't experience those benefits.

00:48:48.377 --> 00:48:55.910
&gt;&gt; That's a great question and, in fact,
I tried to cover this in the book.

00:48:55.910 --> 00:49:02.890
And two weeks ago,
we had a clinic in Harlem.

00:49:02.890 --> 00:49:04.690
We took over a church.

00:49:04.690 --> 00:49:08.150
And invited people from
the community to come in.

00:49:08.150 --> 00:49:13.500
And we had a group of doctors, Dr.
Oz actually filmed it and it was an entire

00:49:13.500 --> 00:49:20.420
hour on his show last week, to talk about
the health and their sleep specifically.

00:49:21.810 --> 00:49:27.700
And what is fascinating is that
the delusions that we talked about,

00:49:27.700 --> 00:49:33.930
that permeated the top,
have actually permeated also

00:49:33.930 --> 00:49:39.550
the bottom socio-economic class and

00:49:39.550 --> 00:49:44.540
unfortunately with even worse
information being given to them.

00:49:44.540 --> 00:49:46.340
I talked to multiple people.

00:49:47.340 --> 00:49:52.800
Most of them significantly overweight and
a lot of them suffering from diabetes.

00:49:52.800 --> 00:49:56.500
And we're talking about
people in their 30s and 40s.

00:49:56.500 --> 00:50:01.240
And most of them told me the same story.

00:50:01.240 --> 00:50:06.620
I have two jobs, I have three jobs,
I sleep for four hours a night.

00:50:06.620 --> 00:50:11.170
And I really blame myself
everyday because I'm exhausted.

00:50:11.170 --> 00:50:13.782
And they thought that if they
were a Wall Street high flyer,

00:50:13.782 --> 00:50:15.210
then wouldn't be exhausted.

00:50:15.210 --> 00:50:20.295
So that's how they had
interpreted our delusion.

00:50:20.295 --> 00:50:25.170
And when

00:50:25.170 --> 00:50:29.560
you sort of ask more question, what would
happen, let me just give you a one story.

00:50:29.560 --> 00:50:36.030
This was a woman in her early 30s
was a pastry chef and a babysitter.

00:50:36.030 --> 00:50:39.940
And she said I would come
home after my jobs and

00:50:39.940 --> 00:50:42.920
that was my time to watch my shows.

00:50:44.030 --> 00:50:48.930
So that was her reward and
what she didn't realize is

00:50:48.930 --> 00:50:53.550
that she was short changing herself of the
one thing that was freely available and

00:50:53.550 --> 00:50:56.460
that she desperately needed,
and that was sleep.

00:50:56.460 --> 00:50:59.450
So sometimes, she said,
I would watch for four hours and

00:50:59.450 --> 00:51:01.570
then fall asleep with the tv.

00:51:01.570 --> 00:51:04.780
Then the tv would wake her up and
then she said I would be so tired.

00:51:04.780 --> 00:51:10.210
But yet I couldn't go back to sleep so I
would go and have something sweet to eat.

00:51:10.210 --> 00:51:13.040
So the vicious cycle was perpetuated.

00:51:13.040 --> 00:51:15.350
So there's a lot that needs to be done.

00:51:16.610 --> 00:51:21.310
You know, we need to raise minimum wage,
we need to deal with the dangers of

00:51:22.460 --> 00:51:25.240
technology taking away so many jobs for

